---
scope: src/tools/upgrade
description: "Agent-driven interactive upgrade that detects per-category drift between a host project's installed methodology artifacts and canonical AIDE, applying only what the user confirms."
intent: >
  aide_upgrade closes the gap that aide_init's never-overwrite stance leaves
  open. When canonical doctrine evolves, host projects drift silently because
  init refuses to clobber existing files. Upgrade is the controlled,
  interactive complement: it surfaces drift the agent walks the user through
  per category, and applies only the categories the user confirms. The flow
  is consent-first by design — the user sees what would change before
  anything is written, and a category is the unit of confirmation, not an
  individual file.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Drift detection runs read-only and groups results by methodology category, so the agent can present each category to the user as a discrete decision.
    - Application happens only on per-category user confirmation, and a confirmed category is applied as a unit — partial application within a category is not offered, preventing mixed-version artifacts.
    - The tool is the executor for files it owns, so the agent never sees canonical content for those categories and cannot hallucinate it; the agent's role is consent-gathering and surfacing results.
    - Files outside the methodology surface are invisible to the tool. User code and user-authored intent specs are never read, compared, or written.
    - The brain step reports missing required sections, existing sections whose text is a known outdated template version, and a legacy brain launcher (unpinned, unversioned, missing auto-confirm, or older than the bundled pin) as distinct kinds of brain drift, and routes the user to the brain configuration command. The upgrade never writes the user-owned brain config, and custom launchers are left unflagged.
    - The MCP step judges each server entry against the canonical shape for the launch mode that entry already uses, so a global entry stays global and a local entry stays local.
  undesired:
    - An apply path that fires without per-category user confirmation, or that treats the whole upgrade as one all-or-nothing decision. Either silently destroys customizations the user would have kept.
    - The tool returning canonical file content to the agent for the agent to write itself. This invites hallucinated content and bypasses the tool-as-executor contract.
    - A drift-detection pass that mutates the host's disk. Comparison must be observably read-only; any side effect during detection is a regression.
    - The tool reading, comparing, or writing files outside the methodology surface — user code, user intent specs, or any artifact aide_init did not install.
    - The brain step writing anything into the user-owned brain config, or reporting brain drift as a generic mismatch that folds stale sections or a legacy launcher into the missing-section report without naming each and where to repair it. Flagging a custom launcher or a customized section as legacy is equally a regression.
    - An upgrade that proposes switching a server entry between global and local launch, or reports a correctly wired global entry as drift.
---
